    def requires_sample(func):
        """
        Another creation by claude. I actually had no idea that this is what those decorator things looked like in
        implementation. I definately think that the best way to try using AI tools is to have it as a refactoring and 
        debugging assistant.
        """
        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
            if curr_sample is None:
                renpy.notify("Select a sample first.")
                return
            return func(*args, **kwargs)
        return wrapper
    
    
    """
    The code below was generated by claude. The key advantage it has over my implementation is that by utilizing higher order functions it 
    centralizes the logic that controls the act of adding a solvent making it easier to introduce new solvents. If I want to extend this to
    other lab procedures, then this design is far better.
    
    @requires_sample
    def add_solvent(solvent):
        value = int('0' + renpy.input(default='0', allow=['1','2','3','4','5','6','7','8','9','0'], length=4, prompt="How many microliters would you like to add?"))
        if not curr_sample.has_space(value):
            return "Not enough space in your tube!"
        curr_sample.add_solvent(value, solvent)
        return None

    def make_solvent_action(key):
        def action():
            message = renpy.invoke_in_new_context(add_solvent, SOLVENTS[key])
            if message:
                renpy.notify(message)   # runs back in the screen's own context
        return action
    
    
    Im going to frankenstein the two approaches together so that I can have the broad application of the add_solvent (that I have built much of my code around)
    with the unique individual behaviour.
    use_ethanol   = make_solvent_action("ethanol")
    use_bufferAE  = make_solvent_action("bufferAE")
    use_PBS       = make_solvent_action("PBS")
    use_proteaseK = make_solvent_action("proteaseK")
    use_bufferAL  = make_solvent_action("bufferAL")
    use_bufferAW1 = make_solvent_action("bufferAW1")
    use_bufferAW2 = make_solvent_action("bufferAW2")
    The Functions below have been replaced by the implementation above.
    """

